        <div class="arb-panel"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb7_1.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over mouth: the deterministic shape under the hand." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-panel sb"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb7_2.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over mouth: the Shape-Bayes posterior with 2σ ellipses over the hidden mouth and jaw." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-panel"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb7_3.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over mouth: landmarks under the hand coloured olive, visible landmarks pink." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-label"><b>Hand over right eye</b><span>23 of 68 landmarks with σ′ &gt; σ₀</span></div>
        <div class="arb-panel"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb8_1.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over right eye: the deterministic shape under the hand." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-panel sb"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb8_2.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over right eye: the Shape-Bayes posterior with 2σ ellipses over the hidden eye, brow and jaw side." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-panel"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb8_3.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over right eye: the hidden eye, brow and jaw side coloured olive, visible landmarks pink." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-label"><b>Hand over left eye</b><span>22 of 68 landmarks with σ′ &gt; σ₀</span></div>
        <div class="arb-panel"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb9_1.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over left eye: the deterministic shape under the hand." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-panel sb"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb9_2.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over left eye: the Shape-Bayes posterior with 2σ ellipses over the hidden eye, brow and jaw side." loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="arb-panel"><img src="assets/fig/live/arb9_3.webp" width="600" height="600" alt="Hand over left eye: the hidden eye, brow and jaw side coloured olive, visible landmarks pink." loading="lazy"></div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 2</span><b>Evidence and prior, weighted per landmark.</b> One subject covers her mouth, her right eye and her left eye. All panels are model outputs: HR-noSA with distilled aleatoric uncertainty σ′, followed by the closed-form Shape-Bayes update. <b>Left:</b> the deterministic prediction, a single shape with no uncertainty estimate. <b>Middle:</b> the posterior mean; ellipses are each landmark's 2σ posterior contour (86% region; covariance scaled ×5 for visibility), drawn where large enough to see. <b>Right:</b> each landmark is coloured by its relative evidence weight <span class="nowrap"><i>W</i>/(<i>W</i> + <i>W</i>₀)</span>, with <span class="nowrap"><i>W</i> = 1/σ′²</span> and <span class="nowrap"><i>W</i>₀ = 1/σ₀²</span>, σ₀ = 4% of the inter-ocular distance. Pink landmarks are determined mainly by their own image evidence; olive landmarks, under the hand, are determined mainly by the shape model, that is, the prior combined with the visible landmarks. AI-generated photographs.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="abstract measure">
    <h4>Abstract</h4>
    <p>Perceiving structured shapes, such as human faces, from pixels is an inherently ambiguous task in real-world conditions. Yet shape inference is largely posed as a deterministic regression task predicting fixed spatial coordinates. We find that deterministic regression is brittle when visual evidence is ambiguous or incomplete: under severe occlusions, deterministic models exhibit structural collapse, predicting incoherent shapes or reverting to generic averages. To address this, we introduce <strong>Shape-Bayes</strong>, a probabilistic framework that couples uncertainty-aware visual perception with Bayesian shape reasoning. Rather than forcing point estimates, Shape-Bayes dynamically weights visual evidence against geometric priors to infer a structurally valid shape posterior. Demonstrated on human face shape regression, a rigorous testbed featuring complex non-rigid deformations and strict anatomical constraints, Shape-Bayes comprises (1) a base model predicting noisy landmarks alongside distilled aleatoric uncertainties; (2) a lightweight Transformer encoding these observations into an adaptive prior over a PCA shape manifold; and (3) a differentiable Bayesian solver computing closed-form posteriors by balancing the noisy predictions against this prior. By guaranteeing complete structural integrity, Shape-Bayes achieves an absolute improvement of up to ~34% IDR over state-of-the-art deterministic models. Simultaneously, it yields highly calibrated uncertainty bounds and reduces relative error by up to 12.5%, establishing a new state of the art for robust 2D face shape regression under severe occlusion.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="stats full" aria-label="Key results">
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="v"><span class="to">100%</span></div>
      <div class="k"><b>In-distribution rate</b> for all six base models on all three occluded benchmarks. HR-noSA alone reaches 66.6% on 300W.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="v">7.56<span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">1.97</span></div>
      <div class="k"><b>Expected calibration error</b> of HR-noSA on occluded 300W, after the Bayesian update.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="v"><span class="to">−21.2%</span></div>
      <div class="k"><b>Relative error on occluded landmarks</b> for PiPNet on 300W, with failure rate down 13.7 points.</div>
    </div>
    <div class="stat">
      <div class="v">9.03<span class="arrow">→</span><span class="to">6.60</span></div>
      <div class="k"><b>minNME over 100 posterior samples</b> versus the deterministic prediction, on occluded landmarks of occluded 300W (oracle selection).</div>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ================= SAMPLING ================= -->
<section class="sec mt-l" id="sampling">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head measure">
      <div class="eyebrow">Posterior sampling</div>
      <h2>A distribution over shapes, <em>not a single estimate</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="measure prose">
      <p>When a burger covers the mouth, the image does not determine whether the mouth is open or closed. A deterministic regressor still returns one shape and no measure of its reliability. Shape-Bayes returns a Gaussian posterior over the PCA shape coefficients, in closed form. Its principal directions of variance correspond to the shape variation that the image leaves undetermined: in this example, moving along the leading direction opens and closes the hidden mouth while the visible eyes stay fixed.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="full mt-m">
      <div class="hyp-rows">
        <div class="hyp-row">
          <div class="hyp"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/burgerS_input.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Input. Burger hides the mouth and jaw; the box marks the zoomed region." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k">Input</span>Burger hides the mouth and jaw; the box marks the zoomed region.</div></div>
          <div class="hyp"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/burgerS_det.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Deterministic prediction, zoomed: a tangled mouth behind the burger." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-gold">Deterministic</span>Single estimate, distorted under the occluder</div></div>
          <div class="hyp sbh"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/burgerS_samples.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Twelve Shape-Bayes posterior samples: they spread only where the face is hidden." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-post">12 posterior samples</span>Variation concentrated in the occluded region</div></div>
          <div class="hyp sbh"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/burgerS_pair.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Two Shape-Bayes hypotheses overlaid, cyan and white. Mouth closed versus mouth open." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-post">Two hypotheses · <span class="sw-c">cyan</span> vs <span class="sw-w">white</span></span>Mouth closed vs open: inner-lip gap 1.0% vs 9.8% of the inter-ocular distance. The eyes move 0.1%.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hyp-row">
          <div class="hyp"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/vrS_input.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Input. Headset hides the eyes and brows; the box marks the zoomed region." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k">Input</span>Headset hides the eyes and brows; the box marks the zoomed region.</div></div>
          <div class="hyp"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/vrS_det.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Deterministic prediction, zoomed: crossed, broken eyes and brows behind the headset." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-gold">Deterministic</span>Single estimate, distorted under the occluder</div></div>
          <div class="hyp sbh"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/vrS_samples.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Twelve Shape-Bayes posterior samples: they spread only where the face is hidden." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-post">12 posterior samples</span>Variation concentrated in the occluded region</div></div>
          <div class="hyp sbh"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/vrS_pair.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Two Shape-Bayes hypotheses overlaid, cyan and white. Brows and eyes in different positions." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-post">Two hypotheses · <span class="sw-c">cyan</span> vs <span class="sw-w">white</span></span>Brows and eyes shift by 6.1% and 3.9% of the inter-ocular distance. The visible mouth moves 0.1%.</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="hyp-row">
          <div class="hyp"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/popcornS_input.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Input. The popcorn tub hides the mouth and chin; the box marks the zoomed region." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k">Input</span>Tub hides the mouth and chin; the box marks the zoomed region.</div></div>
          <div class="hyp"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/popcornS_det.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Deterministic prediction, zoomed: a tangled mouth behind the popcorn tub." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-gold">Deterministic</span>Single estimate, distorted under the occluder</div></div>
          <div class="hyp sbh"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/popcornS_samples.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Twelve Shape-Bayes posterior samples: they spread only where the face is hidden." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-post">12 posterior samples</span>Variation concentrated in the occluded region</div></div>
          <div class="hyp sbh"><div class="hyp-img"><img src="assets/fig/live/popcornS_pair.webp" width="720" height="720" alt="Two Shape-Bayes hypotheses overlaid: mouth open in cyan, mouth closed in white." loading="lazy"></div><div class="hyp-cap"><span class="hyp-k c-post">Two hypotheses · <span class="sw-c">cyan</span> vs <span class="sw-w">white</span></span>Mouth open vs closed: inner-lip gap 11.7% vs 2.5% of the inter-ocular distance. The eyes move 0.1%.</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 3</span><b>Posterior samples and principal-direction hypotheses.</b> Each row is zoomed to the occluded region (box on the input). Samples are drawn from the closed-form Gaussian posterior. The two hypotheses are the posterior mean displaced by ±2σ along the posterior principal direction that moves the occluded landmarks most. Displacements are reported relative to the inter-ocular distance. AI-generated faces; HR-noSA base predictions; covariance scaled ×5 for visibility.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="two wide mt-l" style="align-items:center">
      <div class="stack">
        <h3>Posterior samples include shapes closer to the ground truth</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>On occluded 300W, the posterior sample closest to the ground truth among 100 has an error of 6.60, against 9.03 for the deterministic prediction and 7.91 for the posterior mean. The minimum keeps decreasing with the number of samples, to below 6 at 1,000 (Fig. 9). Because selection uses the ground truth, this measures the quality of the hypothesis set, not of a deployable estimator. Realising the gain requires an external signal that discriminates between hypotheses, such as later video frames, a second view or a task-specific verifier.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="ladder" aria-label="Error on hidden landmarks, occluded 300W, HR-noSA base model">
        <h4>Error on hidden landmarks (NME) ↓</h4>
        <div class="rung"><span class="rl">Deterministic prediction</span><span class="rb"><i class="base" style="width:100%"></i></span><span class="rv">9.03</span></div>
        <div class="rung"><span class="rl">Shape-Bayes posterior mean</span><span class="rb"><i style="width:87.6%"></i></span><span class="rv">7.91</span></div>
        <div class="rung"><span class="rl">Best of 100 posterior samples</span><span class="rb"><i style="width:73.1%"></i></span><span class="rv">6.60</span></div>
        <p class="table-note">Occluded 300W, HR-noSA base model. Best-of-N picks the sample using the ground truth, so it measures how good the hypothesis set is.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="full mt-l stack">
      <h3>Possible uses of posterior samples</h3>
      <div class="apps">
        <div class="app">
          <div class="app-k">Downstream analysis</div>
          <h4 class="app-h">Propagating shape uncertainty</h4>
          <p>Expression and action-unit analysis, affect recognition and visual speech depend on the mouth region. Evaluating such models on posterior samples and aggregating their outputs propagates shape uncertainty into the downstream prediction, instead of conditioning it on a single estimated shape.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="app">
          <div class="app-k">Video</div>
          <h4 class="app-h">Temporal disambiguation</h4>
          <p>Samples can initialise multi-hypothesis or particle-filter trackers. When the occluder moves in later frames, new observations reweight the hypotheses, rather than the track being committed to a single early estimate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="app">
          <div class="app-k">Safety and reliability</div>
          <h4 class="app-h">Failure detection</h4>
          <p>In driver monitoring with a hand on the face, or AR/VR with a headset over the eyes, posterior variance can serve as a rejection signal for deferring a decision or requesting another frame. The paper's sparsification analysis (Fig. 10c) supports this use.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="app">
          <div class="app-k">Selection and labelling</div>
          <h4 class="app-h">Candidate selection</h4>
          <p>A verifier, a multi-view consistency check or a human annotator can select among several shapes that all lie on the shape manifold, rather than correcting a single distorted estimate.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="table-note">These uses are not evaluated in the paper, which measures hypothesis quality with minNME and calibration metrics.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= PROBLEM ================= -->
<section class="sec tint" id="problem">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head measure">
      <div class="eyebrow">The problem</div>
      <h2>Under occlusion, deterministic regressors <em>collapse</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="measure prose">
      <p>Deep regressors are highly accurate on unoccluded faces. But when a region has no meaningful visual evidence, a model that must output one point per landmark still has to guess. It either draws a topologically invalid shape or falls back to a generic average. Even a state-of-the-art model such as HR-noSA keeps only <strong>66.6%</strong> of its 300W predictions inside the valid shape space once faces are occluded.</p>
      <p>Even when its guess is plausible, a deterministic model gives one answer where the image supports several, and nothing tells the user that the answer was a guess.</p>
      <p>Drag the dividers to compare the same images: the deterministic base model on the left, the Shape-Bayes posterior with its uncertainty on the right.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="compare-grid full mt-m">
      <figure class="compare" data-compare>
        <img src="assets/fig/pairs/pair1_base.webp" width="1000" height="979" alt="HR-noSA landmarks on a face partly covered by a baseball glove; the mouth and nose contours are tangled." loading="lazy">
        <div class="top"><img src="assets/fig/pairs/pair1_sb.webp" width="1000" height="979" alt="Shape-Bayes posterior on the same face: a coherent face shape with uncertainty halos around hidden landmarks." loading="lazy"></div>
        <span class="tag l"><i style="background:var(--base)"></i>Deterministic</span>
        <span class="tag r"><i style="background:var(--post)"></i>Shape-Bayes</span>
        <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" aria-label="Reveal Shape-Bayes result, glove example">
        <span class="bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="knob"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 6l-6 6 6 6M15 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></span>
      </figure>
      <figure class="compare" data-compare>
        <img src="assets/fig/pairs/pair2_base.webp" width="1000" height="964" alt="HR-noSA landmarks on a face wearing a VR headset and partly covered by a hand; eyes and brows are distorted." loading="lazy">
        <div class="top"><img src="assets/fig/pairs/pair2_sb.webp" width="1000" height="964" alt="Shape-Bayes posterior on the same face: plausible eyes, brows and jaw, with wide uncertainty under the headset." loading="lazy"></div>
        <span class="tag l"><i style="background:var(--base)"></i>Deterministic</span>
        <span class="tag r"><i style="background:var(--post)"></i>Shape-Bayes</span>
        <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" aria-label="Reveal Shape-Bayes result, VR headset example">
        <span class="bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="knob"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 6l-6 6 6 6M15 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></span>
      </figure>
      <figure class="compare" data-compare>
        <img src="assets/fig/pairs/pair3_base.webp" width="1000" height="990" alt="HR-noSA landmarks on a face covered by a burger; the mouth contour is flattened and displaced." loading="lazy">
        <div class="top"><img src="assets/fig/pairs/pair3_sb.webp" width="1000" height="990" alt="Shape-Bayes posterior on the same face: a natural mouth and jaw inferred behind the burger." loading="lazy"></div>
        <span class="tag l"><i style="background:var(--base)"></i>Deterministic</span>
        <span class="tag r"><i style="background:var(--post)"></i>Shape-Bayes</span>
        <input type="range" min="0" max="100" value="50" aria-label="Reveal Shape-Bayes result, burger example">
        <span class="bar" aria-hidden="true"><span class="knob"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 6l-6 6 6 6M15 6l6 6-6 6"/></svg></span></span>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <p class="full table-note">Occluded 300W test images. Left of each divider: HR-noSA (Yang &amp; Yeh, 2025). Right: the Shape-Bayes posterior mean with per-landmark uncertainty.</p>
    <figure class="full mt-l">
    <div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/abstract_figure.webp" width="2400" height="733" alt="Left: a deterministic regressor's face landmarks collapse under a VR headset. Right: Shape-Bayes predicts uncertainties, infers a posterior mean with covariance, and samples several plausible shapes."></div>
    <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 4</span><b>Robust Bayesian shape inference under severe occlusion.</b> Deterministic regression collapses structurally when forced to predict point estimates for hidden regions (left). Shape-Bayes reconciles noisy visual evidence with a latent shape prior through aleatoric uncertainty and infers a calibrated posterior. Every output is a structurally valid shape, and several plausible hypotheses can be sampled (right).</figcaption>
  </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= IDEA ================= -->
<section class="sec" id="idea">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head measure">
      <div class="eyebrow">The idea</div>
      <h2>Arbitrate between evidence and prior, <em>landmark by landmark</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="measure prose">
      <p>Human vision has long been described as Bayesian inference: incomplete, noisy bottom-up observations are reconciled with robust top-down structural priors. Shape-Bayes makes this explicit for shape regression. A base model reports not just <em>where</em> each landmark is, but <em>how sure</em> it is. That uncertainty acts as a spatial gate in a closed-form Bayesian update, deciding how much each coordinate can move the shape.</p>
    </div>
    <figure class="wide mt-m">
      <div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/intro_schematic.webp" width="1600" height="593" alt="Schematic: an input image yields an observed shape S′ with uncertainty σ′. This defines a likelihood with precision W and, through a learned prior, a precision Λ. A closed-form Bayesian update combines them into the posterior P(S)." loading="lazy"></div>
      <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 5</span><b>Schematic overview.</b> Given an initial noisy shape estimate, Shape-Bayes balances point-wise observation likelihoods (<span class="c-lik">W</span>) against a data-driven structural prior (<span class="c-pri">Λ</span>) through a closed-form Bayesian update, recovering a calibrated posterior distribution <span class="c-post">P(S)</span>.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="two r wide mt-l">
      <div class="stack">
        <h3>Posterior precision is a sum of information</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Shapes live on a PCA manifold, <span class="nowrap">\(\mathbf{S} = \bar{\mathbf{S}} + \mathbf{P}\mathbf{b}\)</span>. With a Gaussian likelihood and a Gaussian prior over the coefficients <span class="nowrap">\(\mathbf{b}\)</span>, the posterior is exactly Gaussian. Its precision is the information from the image plus the information from the prior.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="equation" aria-label="Posterior precision and mean equations">
        $$\tilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_b^{-1} \;=\; \underbrace{\htmlClass{lik}{\mathbf{P}^{\top}\mathbf{W}\,\mathbf{P}}}_{\text{observation}} \;+\; \underbrace{\htmlClass{pri}{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\text{prior}}}}_{\text{prior}}$$
        $$\htmlClass{post}{\mathbf{b}_{\mu}} \;=\; \tilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_b\,\mathbf{P}^{\top}\htmlClass{lik}{\mathbf{W}}\,(\mathbf{s}' - \bar{\mathbf{S}})$$
        $$\htmlClass{lik}{\mathbf{W}} = \operatorname{diag}\!\Big(\frac{1}{\boldsymbol{\sigma}'^{2} + \epsilon}\Big)$$
        <div class="eq-legend">
          <span><i class="sw" style="background:var(--lik)"></i>Evidence from the image</span>
          <span><i class="sw" style="background:var(--pri)"></i>Learned shape prior</span>
          <span><i class="sw" style="background:var(--post)"></i>Posterior</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="limits wide">
      <div class="limit occ">
        <h4>Where the face is hidden</h4>
        <p>\(\sigma' \to \infty\), so \(\mathbf{W} \to 0\). The observation term contributes no information there, and those landmarks are determined by the <span class="c-pri">prior</span> together with the visible landmarks. The estimate remains on the PCA shape manifold by construction.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="limit clean">
        <h4>Where the face is visible</h4>
        <p>\(\sigma' \to 0\), so \(\mathbf{W}\) dominates. Shape-Bayes becomes almost an identity mapping, so accuracy is kept: 300W NME goes from 2.86 to 2.81 on clean images.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= METHOD ================= -->
<section class="sec tint" id="method">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head measure">
      <div class="eyebrow">Method</div>
      <h2>Three components, <em>one exact update</em></h2>
    </div>
    <figure class="full">
      <div class="scroll-x"><div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/method_diagram.webp" width="2400" height="688" alt="Inference flow: an uncertainty-aware base regressor outputs landmarks and uncertainties; these define a likelihood precision W and, through a Transformer, a prior precision Λ in PCA space; a closed-form Bayesian update yields the posterior N(b_μ, Σ_b), from which shapes are sampled and projected." loading="lazy"></div></div>
      <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 6</span><b>Inference flow.</b> (1) A base regression model predicts landmarks S′ and uncertainties σ′, defining the likelihood precision <span class="c-lik">W</span>. (2) A Transformer maps these observations to an adaptive prior precision <span class="c-pri">Λ<sub>prior</sub></span> in PCA space. (3) A closed-form Bayesian update computes the shape posterior <span class="c-post">𝒩(b<sub>μ</sub>, Σ̃<sub>b</sub>)</span>; probable shapes are obtained by sampling and projecting, S = S̄ + Pb.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="steps full mt-l">
      <!-- Step 1 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-n">Step 1</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <div class="two l">
            <figure class="tall-fig">
              <div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/distilled_uncert.webp" width="1100" height="1495" alt="Distillation pipeline: a masked face is rotated from −45° to 45°, a frozen teacher predicts landmarks for each view, predictions are inverse-transformed back, and their spread gives per-landmark uncertainty, large around the mask." loading="lazy"></div>
              <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 7</span><b>Distilled uncertainty supervision.</b> Teacher predictions on affine-augmented views are mapped back to the original frame; their spread is the aleatoric target.</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <div class="stack">
              <h3>Uncertainty from visual feature stability</h3>
              <div class="prose">
                <p>A visible, salient region has robust local features: its predicted coordinates barely move when the image is rotated, scaled or blurred. An occluded region has no discriminative evidence, so the model extrapolates from weak global context, and those guesses scatter under the same transformations.</p>
                <p>Shape-Bayes turns this into supervision without manual labels. A frozen teacher predicts shapes for <em>M</em> augmented views. The predictions are warped back with the inverse transforms, and their root-mean-square deviation from the unaugmented prediction becomes the target uncertainty:</p>
              </div>
              <div class="equation">$$\boldsymbol{\sigma}'_{\text{target}} = \Big(\tfrac{1}{M}\textstyle\sum_{i=1}^{M} \big(\mathbf{S}_i - \mathbf{S}'_{\text{target}}\big)^2\Big)^{1/2}$$</div>
              <div class="prose"><p>The base model learns to output coordinates and uncertainties together, so at inference it needs <strong>a single forward pass</strong>. The scheme works with any architecture: heatmap, coordinate or Transformer regressors. Models that already predict uncertainty, such as LUVLi, need no distillation at all.</p></div>
              <div class="spec"><span>M = 30 views</span><span>rotation ±35°</span><span>scale 1.1–1.4</span><span>blur · flips</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 2 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-n">Step 2</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>A prior that adapts to the input</h3>
          <div class="two">
            <div class="prose">
              <p>A static PCA prior is too rigid for large deformations such as head pose. Shape-Bayes uses a lightweight Transformer that reads the observation itself: each of the <em>N</em> landmarks is one token, \(\mathbf{X} = [\mathbf{S}', \boldsymbol{\sigma}'] \in \mathbb{R}^{N\times 4}\).</p>
              <p>Through self-attention, confident visible regions (the eyes) inform the structural placement of hidden ones (a covered jaw). The encoder outputs the diagonal of the <span class="c-pri">prior precision</span> over the latent shape coefficients:</p>
            </div>
            <div class="stack">
              <div class="equation">$$\htmlClass{pri}{\boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\text{prior}}} = \operatorname{diag}\big(\exp(E_{\phi}(\mathbf{X}))\big) \in \mathbb{R}^{K\times K}$$
                <p class="eq-note">Prior \(\mathcal{N}(\mathbf{0},\, \boldsymbol{\Lambda}_{\text{prior}}^{-1})\) over \(\mathbf{b}\). The exponential keeps it strictly positive.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="spec"><span>5 encoder layers</span><span>d = 64</span><span>8 heads</span><span>429K parameters</span><span>PCA rank K captures &gt;99% variance</span></div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Step 3 -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-n">Step 3</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>A differentiable, closed-form Bayesian solver</h3>
          <div class="two">
            <div class="prose">
              <p>Likelihood times prior gives an exact Gaussian posterior over shape coefficients. Because the manifold is low-dimensional, inverting the <em>K</em>×<em>K</em> precision is trivial and fully differentiable, so the whole module trains end to end.</p>
              <p>The posterior mean is the predicted shape. Samples from the posterior are alternative shapes; each lies on the PCA shape manifold by construction.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="equation">
              $$p(\mathbf{b}\mid\mathbf{s}',\mathbf{X}) = \mathcal{N}\big(\htmlClass{post}{\mathbf{b}_{\mu}},\, \htmlClass{post}{\tilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_b}\big)$$
              $$\mathbf{b} \sim \mathcal{N}\big(\mathbf{b}_{\mu}, \tilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_b\big) \;\Rightarrow\; \mathbf{S} = \bar{\mathbf{S}} + \mathbf{P}\mathbf{b}$$
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Training -->
      <div class="step">
        <div class="step-n">Training</div>
        <div class="step-body">
          <h3>Learning <em>where</em> and <em>how sure</em>, separately</h3>
          <div class="two">
            <div class="prose">
              <p>With the base model frozen and the PCA basis precomputed, only the prior Transformer is trained, on occlusion-augmented shapes. An MSE term on the posterior mean anchors coordinates. A negative log-likelihood term calibrates the posterior.</p>
              <p class="callout">A stop-gradient on the mean inside the NLL decouples the two: <b>MSE drives the where</b>, <b>NLL drives the how sure</b>. The model cannot lower its loss by shifting the mean instead of fixing its variance.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="equation">
              $$\mathcal{L} = \lambda_{\text{MSE}}\,\lVert \mathbf{b}_{\mu} - \mathbf{b}_{\text{gt}} \rVert^2 + \lambda_{\text{NLL}}\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}}$$
              $$\begin{aligned}\mathcal{L}_{\text{NLL}} = {}& \tfrac{1}{2}(\mathbf{b}_{\text{gt}} - \bar{\mathbf{b}}_{\mu})^{\top}\tilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_b^{-1}(\mathbf{b}_{\text{gt}} - \bar{\mathbf{b}}_{\mu}) \\ & - \tfrac{1}{2}\log\det\tilde{\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}_b^{-1}\end{aligned}$$
              <p class="eq-note">\(\bar{\mathbf{b}}_{\mu} = \operatorname{sg}[\mathbf{b}_{\mu}]\) is a stop-gradient on the mean.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <figure class="full mt-l">
      <div class="scroll-x"><div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/bayes_shape_flow_qual.webp" width="2400" height="486" alt="One masked face through the pipeline: input image, base model shape prediction with errors, its uncertainty, the Bayesian posterior, two posterior samples with open and closed mouth, and an ensemble of five samples." loading="lazy"></div></div>
      <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 8</span><b>One image through the pipeline.</b> Input, base model prediction and its aleatoric uncertainty, Bayesian posterior mean, and shapes sampled from the posterior. Under the mask, the posterior keeps both an open-mouth and a closed-mouth hypothesis, which the image cannot decide between.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= RESULTS ================= -->
<section class="sec" id="results">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head measure">
      <div class="eyebrow">Results</div>
      <h2>Structural validity across base models, <em>with lower error under occlusion</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="measure prose">
      <p>Shape-Bayes is evaluated on the 300W, WFLW and COFW test sets under a dynamic occlusion protocol: synthetic wearable masks and a library of more than 80 real-world objects overlaid on the images, without changing the ground-truth shapes. It is attached to six state-of-the-art base models spanning heatmap, coordinate and Transformer paradigms.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="full mt-m stack" id="xarch">
      <div class="toolbar">
        <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Benchmark">
          <button role="tab" aria-selected="true" data-ds="300W">300W</button>
          <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-ds="WFLW">WFLW</button>
          <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" data-ds="COFW">COFW</button>
        </div>
        <div class="legend"><span><i class="dot base"></i>Base model</span><span><i class="dot sb"></i>+ Shape-Bayes</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="charts">
        <div class="chart-card">
          <h4>In-distribution rate (IDR) ↑</h4>
          <p class="sub">% of predicted shapes inside the 95% hyperellipsoid of the shape manifold</p>
          <svg class="chart" id="chart-idr" role="img" aria-label="In-distribution rate per base model, before and after Shape-Bayes"></svg>
        </div>
        <div class="chart-card">
          <h4>Error on occluded landmarks (NME<sub>occ</sub>) ↓</h4>
          <p class="sub">Normalized mean error, % of inter-ocular distance</p>
          <svg class="chart" id="chart-occ" role="img" aria-label="Occluded-landmark error per base model, before and after Shape-Bayes"></svg>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data" id="table-xarch">
          <thead><tr><th>Base model</th><th>IDR ↑</th><th>NME<sub>occ</sub> ↓</th><th>NME<sub>all</sub> ↓</th><th>minNME<sub>@100</sub> ↓</th><th>FR<sub>10</sub> ↓</th><th>AUC<sub>10</sub> ↑</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="table-note">Each cell shows base → +Shape-Bayes, with the relative change for error metrics. <span class="nowrap">NATIVE</span> marks models that already output uncertainty and need no distillation. A dash means the model was not evaluated on that benchmark. minNME<sub>@100</sub> takes the posterior sample closest to the ground truth out of 100, so it measures the quality of the hypothesis set rather than of a single prediction.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="two wide mt-l">
      <div class="stack">
        <h3>Structural integrity, without over-smoothing</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Prior-driven shape regularization usually pays for validity with accuracy on visible landmarks. Here it does not. Visible, confident landmarks act as strong anchors on the latent shape, while uncertain ones are down-weighted and completed by the prior.</p>
          <p>The gains are not traded between regions: on occluded 300W, every facial part improves.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="chart-card">
        <h4>Part-wise NME<sub>occ</sub> on occluded 300W ↓</h4>
        <p class="sub">Base model: HR-noSA</p>
        <svg class="chart" id="chart-parts" role="img" aria-label="Occluded-landmark error by facial region, before and after Shape-Bayes"></svg>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="two wide mt-l">
      <div class="stack">
        <h3>Clean test sets</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Without occlusion, predicted uncertainties vanish, observation precision dominates the prior, and Shape-Bayes reduces to an identity mapping. It preserves or slightly improves base accuracy. Sampling 100 shapes from the posterior and taking the closest one gives the lowest error on all three benchmarks, a sign that the posterior places mass on accurate geometric hypotheses.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>300W</th><th>WFLW</th><th>COFW</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>AWing</td><td>3.07</td><td>4.36</td><td>4.94</td></tr>
            <tr><td>LUVLi</td><td>3.23</td><td>4.37</td><td class="na">–</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ViTPose</td><td>3.02</td><td>4.26</td><td>4.85</td></tr>
            <tr><td>ADNet</td><td>2.93</td><td>4.14</td><td>4.68</td></tr>
            <tr><td>HIH</td><td>3.09</td><td>4.08</td><td>4.63</td></tr>
            <tr><td>SLPT</td><td>3.17</td><td>4.14</td><td>4.79</td></tr>
            <tr><td>STAR</td><td>2.90</td><td>4.03</td><td>4.62</td></tr>
            <tr><td>OccFace</td><td>2.83</td><td class="best">3.77</td><td>4.53</td></tr>
            <tr class="sep"><td>HR-noSA</td><td>2.86</td><td>3.97</td><td>4.53</td></tr>
            <tr class="ours"><td>+ Shape-Bayes (mean)</td><td class="best">2.81</td><td>3.94</td><td class="best">4.50</td></tr>
            <tr class="ours"><td>+ Shape-Bayes (minNME<sub>@100</sub>)</td><td>2.79</td><td>3.72</td><td>4.23</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="wide table-note">NME<sub>all</sub> ↓ on the original test sets. Bold marks the best single prediction per benchmark. The minNME row selects among samples using the ground truth and is reported separately.</p>

    <div class="wide mt-l stack">
      <h3>Ablation: what each component buys</h3>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table class="data ablation">
          <thead><tr><th>Configuration</th><th>NME ↓</th><th>minNME<sub>@100</sub> ↓</th><th>FR ↓</th><th>AUC ↑</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody id="ablation-body"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <div class="two r" style="align-items:center">
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Projecting onto PCA with unweighted least squares barely helps: corrupted landmarks act as heavy outliers that pull confident ones out of place. Weighting by distilled inverse variance turns the projection into Mahalanobis minimization and provides most of the gain. The adaptive prior adds the rest by accommodating input-specific variation such as head pose.</p>
          <p>Because the output is a distribution, sampling more shapes keeps finding better hypotheses, well below the deterministic baseline.</p>
        </div>
        <figure>
          <div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/minNME_Ablation.webp" width="1400" height="883" alt="minNME as a function of the number of posterior samples, from about 8 at 5 samples to about 5.9 at 1000, below the deterministic baseline of 9 for all three variants; Shape-Bayes is lowest throughout." loading="lazy"></div>
          <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 9</span><b>minNME<sub>@N</sub></b> on occluded 300W versus the number of posterior samples <em>N</em>.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
      <p class="table-note">Occluded 300W, HR-noSA base model, metrics on occluded landmarks.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= CALIBRATION ================= -->
<section class="sec tint" id="calibration">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head measure">
      <div class="eyebrow">Calibration</div>
      <h2>Posterior <em>calibration</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="measure prose">
      <p>Under out-of-distribution occlusion, most base models are overconfident: their predicted uncertainty underestimates their error. Propagating the aleatoric uncertainty through the prior-constrained update increases posterior variance where visual evidence is missing, and expected calibration error decreases for all five evaluated architectures.</p>
    </div>

    <figure class="full mt-m">
      <div class="scroll-x"><div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/300w_uncert_calibration.webp" width="2400" height="617" alt="Three calibration plots on occluded 300W: (a) predicted uncertainty versus observed error with Spearman correlation 0.67; (b) reliability diagram close to the diagonal, ECE 1.966; (c) sparsification curve tracking the oracle." loading="lazy"></div></div>
      <figcaption><span class="fig-n">Fig. 10</span><b>Posterior calibration on occluded 300W.</b> (a) Predicted uncertainty correlates with observed error (Spearman ρ = 0.67). (b) The reliability diagram stays close to perfect calibration (ECE = 1.97). (c) Rejecting the most uncertain predictions isolates accurate shapes, and the sparsification curve tracks the oracle.</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="two wide mt-l">
      <div class="chart-card">
        <h4>Expected calibration error (ECE) ↓</h4>
        <p class="sub">Occluded 300W. *LUVLi uses its own native uncertainty.</p>
        <svg class="chart" id="chart-ece" role="img" aria-label="Expected calibration error per base model, before and after Shape-Bayes"></svg>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap" style="align-self:start">
        <table class="data">
          <thead><tr><th>Base model</th><th>NLL ↓</th><th>ECE ↓</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody id="calib-body"></tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= GALLERY ================= -->
<section class="sec" id="gallery">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="sec-head measure">
      <div class="eyebrow">Qualitative results</div>
      <h2>Across benchmarks and <em>occluders</em></h2>
    </div>
    <div class="full stack">
      <div class="toolbar">
        <div class="tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Gallery benchmark">
          <button role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="g-300w" data-g="g-300w">300W</button>
          <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="g-cofw" data-g="g-cofw">COFW</button>
          <button role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="g-wflw" data-g="g-wflw">WFLW</button>
        </div>
        <p class="table-note" style="margin:0">Columns: input · base prediction · base uncertainty · posterior · two samples · 5-sample ensemble. Select to enlarge.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="gallery-panel" id="g-300w" role="tabpanel">
        <div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/300W_QUAL_RESULTS.webp" width="2000" height="2029" alt="Grid of seven occluded 300W faces (popcorn, pizza, burger, fan, ball, glove) through the pipeline from base prediction to posterior samples." loading="lazy"></div>
      </figure>
      <figure class="gallery-panel" id="g-cofw" role="tabpanel" hidden>
        <div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/COFW_QUAL_RESULTS.webp" width="2000" height="3183" alt="Grid of occluded COFW faces through the pipeline from base prediction to posterior samples." loading="lazy"></div>
      </figure>
      <figure class="gallery-panel" id="g-wflw" role="tabpanel" hidden>
        <div class="plate"><img class="zoomable" src="assets/fig/WFLW_QUAL_RESULTS.webp" width="2000" height="2885" alt="Grid of occluded WFLW faces through the pipeline from base prediction to posterior samples." loading="lazy"></div>
      </figure>
      <p class="table-note">HR-noSA (Yang &amp; Yeh, 2025) base model versus Shape-Bayes on dynamically occluded test images.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= EFFICIENCY + SCOPE ================= -->
<section class="sec tint" id="efficiency">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="two wide">
      <div class="stack">
        <div class="eyebrow">Cost</div>
        <h3>Computational cost</h3>
        <div class="prose"><p>The base network stays frozen. Shape-Bayes adds a small Transformer and a <em>K</em>×<em>K</em> solve, so the backbone still dominates runtime.</p></div>
        <div class="facts">
          <div class="fact"><div class="v">429K</div><div class="k">parameters, 3.2% of the base network</div></div>
          <div class="fact"><div class="v">0.10%</div><div class="k">of base FLOPs (~30 MFLOPs vs 29.1 GFLOPs)</div></div>
          <div class="fact"><div class="v">&lt;0.5 ms</div><div class="k">per image on an RTX 3090</div></div>
          <div class="fact"><div class="v">&lt;2 MB</div><div class="k">inference memory, weights included</div></div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="stack">
        <div class="eyebrow">Scope</div>
        <h3>Beyond faces</h3>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Faces are the testbed here: extreme rigid and non-rigid deformation, strict anatomical constraints, and abundant occlusion. Nothing in the solver itself is specific to faces. It needs a set of landmarks, their uncertainties and a shape basis, so the framework is topology-agnostic.</p>
          <p>Natural next targets are other structured shape problems under ambiguity, such as body and hand pose, anatomical landmarks in medical images, and lane geometry. Those experiments are future work.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

